what's up guys michael here the saying  geek culture as pop culture has become a  cliche and with good reason superheroes  rule the box office video games bring in  more money than movies and all the cool  kids play tabletop rpgs it seems the  mainstream has shaken off its affected  smirk and dived full force into  unabashed nerdum what's more the ways we  interact with pop culture have also  changed reflecting the behaviors of geek  culture that normies once shunned we  used to make fun of trekkies and comic  book fans were roasted for obsessing  over the finer details of continuity now  you can't even watch a movie where cars  go vroom without taking on a laundry  list of prior movies and spin-offs  wanting to play video games for a living  was once equivalent to wanting to be a  power ranger now esports stars make a  good chunk of what professional athletes  make without the concussions and going  to bat for your favorite franchise is  now standard practice i want to talk  about starcraft 2 in many ways the last  hurrah of the single-player rts and if  you ask me the rightful queen of the  genre so how did geek culture become  ubiquitous and does geek culture  actually still exist once it's so  mainstream get yourself comfy for this  wisecrack edition is geek culture dead  since the beginning geek subculture was  concerned with and unified primarily by  media especially genre fiction which  means the geek culture has been  inextricably tied to the concept of  fandom important note geek culture  overlaps with fan culture but doesn't  encompass all fandom basically all geeks  are fans but not all fans are geeks  anyway modern fandom has really been  around since the 1890s when sherlock  holmes captivated the emerging urban  middle class holmes fans even protested  when sir arthur conan doyle killed off  the beloved detective so much so that  the author begrudgingly brought him back  to life while the term fandom was  applied to sports and feeder lovers  throughout the 19th century sci-fi  literature lovers appropriated it as  they developed much of the fannish  infrastructure jargon and language still  used to this day according to scholar  francesca kappa sci-fi fans started  communicating via the letters column of  famed magazine amazing stories soon they  began to produce their own zines  was this geeky behavior for sure but  according to scholar lars konzak geekdom  as we know it today can really be  credited to the culture built by world  war ii veterans especially engineers and  intelligence officers when veterans  returned from war and began their gi  bill funded higher education they had  the time and space to process what  they'd been through as konzak explains  they expressed their reaction to their  unique experience by creating and  embracing science fiction novels  superhero comic books and military  strategy board games this konzak says  coalesced into its own distinctive  alternative intellectual milieu around  the cold war era but modern geeky fandom  was poised to really explode around 1966  when scifi made the jump from books and  movies to the more accessible tv format  and fans of star trek arguably set the  blueprint for geek fandom as we know it  today belittled by the mainstream they  formed a subculture of their own to  express their passion born in part out  of the counterculture movements of the  1960s trekkies were super grassroots and  self-organized they created a dedicated  convention popularized fanfic and  cosplay and some argue got their show a  third season as technology progressed  creators could bring ever more  engrossing worlds to life and then of  course star wars hit in 77 and  spearheaded the now ubiquitous approach  to merchandise by now a fandom had  attracted scholarly attention akademik's  initial reactions to fandom stereotyped  these new fans much like they had sports  fans as being pathological it was even  commonly suggested that fans needed  psychiatric help but that didn't stop by  the 1990s dedicated fandoms had become  pretty commonplace for geeky fiction all  following the grassroots subculture  example set by trekkies the growth of  the internet facilitated greater  connection between fans than the readers  of amazing stories could have ever  imagined by this point it was possible  for people that have been in a fandom  for most of their lives and it was  around this time that fan studies  scholars started pushing back against  prevailing stereotypes most notably  there was henry jenkins an academic and  fellow fan frustrated with the  mainstream stereotype of fandoms as  mindless and anti-social consumers  obsessed with immature stories jenkins  explored fandoms ethnographically he  framed them as communities rooted in  creative engagement with a given media  text creating new meanings to base the  social actions of the subculture key to  this is a conflicting set of emotions  to jenkins fandom is both fascinated  with and frustrated by the media they  focus on they're emotionally invested in  the world of the text but that  investment also breeds frustration with  the text inevitable shortcomings this  fuels acts of creation like fan fiction  and art also known as fan labor through  acts of fan labor fandoms are  essentially tailoring the text to  reflect their own tastes views and  experiences although it's not just  creative interpretation it even extends  to acts of evangelism like when fandoms  organize enmas to save their favorite  shows jenkins labels this participatory  culture where the public has more agency  in the formation and definition of their  culture participatory culture acts as a  sort of grassroots counterpoint to  mindless media consumption but even as  smart folks started defending fans of  geek culture one thing remained the same  the mainstream's scorn according to  scholar dan hassler forest fans had  mainly been perceived by hollywood film  studios as irritants niche groups of  excessively invested consumers whose  limited numbers rendered their voices  insignificant films like tim burton's  1989 film batman were thus purposely  tailored to attract the largest  mainstream audience rather than to pay  homage to fans according to hasler  forest that was about to change with one  big ass franchise lord of the rings  director peter jackson sadly embraced  the simmering fan anticipation for the  film he gave the blogger behind fan  website the one ring a private tour of  the set let her take photographs and  eventually relied on her as a main  source of publicity for the sequels this  marked what hassler forest calls a  shifting relationship between fan  culture and media industries where fans  were addressed not only as the ideal  consumers of a commercial fantastic  franchise but also as valuable  contributors to the process  the commercial success of lord of the  rings fans started being viewed as  influencers who could produce hype that  would ideally bring in an even larger  audience this potential mushroomed as  the internet expanded in media  industries started to rely more and more  on networked cultures and spreadable  media according to hassler forest what's  more performing fannish behavior has  become less taboo which hassler forest  argues is in part due to the radical  individualization that marks our social  media environment as a result a lot more  people are doing fandom and there's  every reason to think fans will continue  to become more important as hollywood  keeps chasing the most profitable ip  some scholars think phantom will become  so mainstream as to render the  distinction between fan and average  audience member virtually insignificant  now mainstream media didn't start  embracing fandom out of the goodness of  their hearts stats show that the most  dedicated fans of tv and movies spend  around 45 more than the rest for gamers  it's  230 percent what's more fans vastly  outnumber casual viewers couple that  with fans zealous dedication to hyping  their favorite franchises and you can  see why no red-blooded exec could  possibly keep ignoring them over the  years geek fandoms have proven to be a  valuable commodity to media corporations  which has meant a fundamental shift in  how fandoms form corporations are now  actively looking to create fandoms from  day one with different tactics for  different stages of fandom all to stir  fans into the most passionate and  profitable state at the same time geek  culture is becoming increasingly  corporatized conventions like new york  comic con and chicago comic and  entertainment expo and so on are being  bought up by read pop a subsidiary of  global events organizer reed exhibitions  which continues to engulf ever more once  grassroots events at the same time film  studios and media conglomerates have  increasingly infiltrated these events  luring fans with exciting exclusive  previews that they'll be sure to tweet  about and now media corporations have  found ways to commodify fandom itself by  exploiting fan labor this takes myriad  forms like competitions where the prize  is your fan art being used as  promotional material or being featured  in the movie that's also known as unpaid  labor or leveraging ip law against free  fan works like short films or game mods  only to release their own versions soon  after even fanfic is being exploited  take shipping the practice of creating  fanfic around theoretical romantic  pairings in an ip historically a way for  queer fans to create their own  representation popular ships are now  being incorporated into marketing and  even alluded to in the text themselves  just to get fans in the door  but often it's a trick these ships are  largely ignored in favor of straight  romances this isn't necessarily the end  of the world but the iron grip  corporations are starting to have on  fandoms arguably creates problems when a  corporation all but controls a  subculture can you really call it a  subculture anymore and what happens when  a subculture used to operate in his  outcast in opposition to the mainstream  finds their forum confined by corporate  oversight similarly subcultures like  those surrounding indie rock punk and  emo music have encountered these same  tensions essentially fandoms used to  play in the woods but corporations have  paved them over with a high fenced  jungle gym the question then poses  itself when fandom is mainstream does it  even really exist anymore fans may be  courted by corporations but do they  actually have power especially when a  few media conglomerates own almost all  of popular culture's ip it depends who  you ask  from jenkins interpretation fandom could  be seen as an active way to invert the  increased consolidation of ip he argued  that fanfic in particular is a way of  the culture repairing the damage done in  a system where contemporary myths are  owned by corporations instead of owned  by the folk this interpretation sees  fans as active creators and consumers of  their favorite geek media rather than  passive receptacles for whatever  corporate media wants them to stand this  is interesting because jenkins  acknowledges that the media that fandoms  form around are essentially corporate  products regardless of the empowering  aspects of jenkins proposed  participatory culture fandoms are still  emotionally invested in something that  corporations make for profit which is  why others argue that the fan autonomy  celebrated by jenkins is something of an  illusion some of this has to do with the  power of the mighty ip as simone murray  puts it the ultimate discretion of media  conglomerates to shut down fan creations  around corporate owned properties  renders hollow much cultural studies  champion of fans contrarian impulse that  is to say when disney by the way of  marvel can sue you for using too many  clips in your reaction video to ant-man  can you actually take culture into your  own hands according to hessler forest  not really he argues that there's a  grossly asymmetrical relationship  between fans and media producers such  that fan autonomy becomes somewhat  illusory he writes that when beloved  stories become highly profitable global  entertainment brands they no longer make  up a form of cultural commons that can  be appropriated embellished transformed  or subverted instead they become  lucrative corporate franchises made up  of a wide range of commodities most of  which are dependent upon fans to achieve  their desired commercial value that is  to say media producers have both the  power to stop fans from circulating  their arts and their resources to  produce a gobsmacking amount of content  and products in both limiting fan  production and aggressively encouraging  fan consumption media producers are  arguably co-opting fan culture while  stymying its expression what this  argument really spotlights is the  tension between the two sides of fandom  fans as anti-commercial subcultures  organized on a grassroots level and fans  as rabid consumers and promoters of  corporate ip and as fandom becomes  increasingly mainstream that tension  might just constitute one of the most  important debates about how we consume  media  but what do you guys think has corporate  involvement changed what it means to be  a fan is there still room for geek  culture let us know in the comments big  thanks to our patrons for all your  support and don't forget to check out  our podcast shove that subscribe button  like it's a nerdith or locker and don't  forget to ring that bell and as always  thanks for watching later  [Music]  you

